WEBVTT

NOTE Draft cue timing follows measured scene audio and word count. Review spoken word alignment before publication.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.576
Welcome to Resgrid Academy. Resgrid brings dispatch, people, apparatus, and department management into

00:00:05.576 --> 00:00:11.152
a connected system. This series explains what each feature does, how to prepare

00:00:11.152 --> 00:00:15.870
it, and how to tell whether it is helping your department.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:22.927
Picture a fictional department with two stations, an engine, and a rescue unit.

00:00:22.927 --> 00:00:29.383
First, administrators organize members, groups, roles, and units. A dispatcher creates a training

00:00:29.383 --> 00:00:35.840
call and selects the intended resources. Members respond through their assigned devices. After

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.800
closeout, officers complete records and follow up on equipment, supplies, and staffing.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.853
Start with the foundation. Set the department address and time zone. Build the

00:00:48.853 --> 00:00:55.306
roster, home stations, personnel roles, and unit types. Review who can dispatch, manage

00:00:55.306 --> 00:01:01.760
information, and administer the department. Prepare account recovery as you set up stronger

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.220
sign-in protections. These choices influence later schedules, recipients, maps, and reports.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:14.472
For operations, learn call intake, types, priorities, recipient routing, run cards, and response

00:01:14.472 --> 00:01:21.123
statuses. Mapping adds location and site context. Messaging and notification rules keep the

00:01:21.123 --> 00:01:27.775
intended audience informed. Verify communications on actual devices. A saved call or provider

00:01:27.775 --> 00:01:32.380
handoff is different from confirmed receipt by a member.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:39.530
Between calls, shifts help plan coverage. Training and certification records make learning and

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:46.080
renewal work visible. Checklists record routine inspections. Maintenance gives defects an owner and

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:52.630
a documented repair process. Inventory records where equipment and supplies are stored, issued,

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:59.180
and replenished. Documents, contacts, protocols, and calendars give members a shared reference point.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:06.675
Records connect incident reporting, prevention, investigation, evidence, review, and disclosure. Deployments keep extended

00:02:06.675 --> 00:02:13.570
response activity and supporting documents together. Business Operations adds commercial and costing workflows

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:20.465
where enabled. Teach the source activity, review decisions, and recorded outcomes together, rather

00:02:20.465 --> 00:02:26.830
than treating an export or prepared claim as a completed external submission.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:33.216
Feature availability depends on the deployed release and department access. Some features need

00:02:33.216 --> 00:02:39.002
an enabled module, a permission, an add-on, or protection enrollment. The source catalog

00:02:39.002 --> 00:02:44.788
marks Enhanced AI as planned. Buying an add-on is separate from configuring it.

00:02:44.788 --> 00:02:50.575
Setup Report helps review configuration evidence; it does not prove that every operational

00:02:50.575 --> 00:02:52.800
workflow or notification has succeeded.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:59.496
Choose the learning path that matches your responsibility: administrator, dispatcher, member, or specialist.

00:02:59.496 --> 00:03:05.592
Each chapter explains a department problem, the relevant features, setup decisions, and a

00:03:05.592 --> 00:03:11.687
practice task. These illustrated lessons are based on the current source. Live screen

00:03:11.687 --> 00:03:17.783
walkthroughs will be recorded against a verified demo department, with the same scenarios

00:03:17.783 --> 00:03:19.190
and feature scripts.

